#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4c7c790-d016-564e-8f2a-a689ca3b0dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302869} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9325d7-f550-5c59-88d7-8ff8a9233ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302870} {:answer/id #uuid "b87282ce-139d-532c-be53-6935258cb3e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-18+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302871} {:answer/id #uuid "b806830b-01c1-5815-b667-6de6e14cfc1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302872} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce38dcf-0d4f-57ad-abc3-15b67f37213c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4c7c790-d016-564e-8f2a-a689ca3b0dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302869} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c96509-f5e7-56e3-8067-f632bbdd7285" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f6300777-605f-507f-978f-04a8f9538fa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-24576" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$"}]}
